<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Mellifont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War (Ireland)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Mellifont was the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone</a>, to Lord Mountjoy at Mellifont in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a> in March 1603, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>. O'Neill renounced his Gaelic title and accepted English law but kept most of his lands; he had not been told that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> had died days earlier. Growing pressure on his position led to the Flight of the Earls in 1607.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-mellifont.jpg" alt="Treaty of Mellifont" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TyroneSubmitstoMountjoy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the English victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kinsale">Battle of Kinsale</a>, Lord Deputy Charles Blount, 8th Baron Mountjoy, knew that Hugh O'Neill, though in hiding, was still a threat: Rory O'Donnell, Brian Oge O'Rourke, Cuchonnacht Maguire and Donal Cam O'Sullivan Beare remained loyal to him. In the spring of 1603 Mountjoy campaigned in the northern counties and Leinster, ordering the land scorched, and famine followed. The war was costing three quarters of the Exchequer's annual revenue and an army of 20,000 men, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> dropped her demand for unconditional surrender.</p><p>Mountjoy's agents were Sir William Godolphin and Sir Garrett Moore, a friend of O'Neill, who found him near Lough Neagh in early March and persuaded him to negotiate under a safe conduct. The talks were held at Mellifont, Moore's seat, bought by his family after the dissolution of the Cistercian abbey. On 27 March Mountjoy learnt that the Queen had died three days earlier, but he kept the news from the others until 5 April. O'Neill submitted on 30 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The terms were thought very generous. In return for giving up the Gaelic title of The O'Neill, his attainder was reversed, restoring him as Earl of Tyrone with a seat in the Irish House of Lords, and he kept his core territory, apart from Church lands, in freehold under English law. He swore loyalty, promised to seek no foreign help and was pardoned. English law was to replace Brehon law on his lands, English was to be the official language, the earls could no longer support the Gaelic bards, and no Catholic colleges could be built on his property. Unlike earlier surrender and regrant agreements, he was not required to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Treaty of London between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1603 ended any hope of Spanish aid. O'Neill went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with Mountjoy in June 1603, and an Act of Oblivion in 1604 pardoned offences committed before King James's accession. Sir Arthur Chichester, Lord Deputy from February 1605, worked to undermine O'Neill's authority and wealth, and after the Flight of the Earls in 1607 seized their lands, opening the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Mellifont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
